[section:turbo Trust Region Bayesian Optimization]

[heading Synopsis]

``
    #include <boost/math/optimization/turbo.hpp>

    namespace boost::math::optimization {

    template <typename ArgumentContainer> struct turbo_parameters {
        using Real = typename ArgumentContainer::value_type;
        ArgumentContainer lower_bounds;
        ArgumentContainer upper_bounds;
        ArgumentContainer const * initial_guess = nullptr;
        size_t max_function_calls = 0;
        size_t initial_points = 0;
        size_t batch_size = 1;
        size_t candidates = 0;
        size_t max_training_points = 200;
        size_t hyperparameter_steps = 50;
    };

    template <typename ArgumentContainer>
    void validate_turbo_parameters(turbo_parameters<ArgumentContainer> const &params);

    template <typename ArgumentContainer, class Func, class URBG>
    ArgumentContainer turbo(const Func cost_function,
                            turbo_parameters<ArgumentContainer> const &params,
                            URBG &gen,
                            std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer> target_value
                              = std::numeric_limits<std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>>::quiet_NaN(),
                            std::atomic<bool> *cancellation = nullptr,
                            std::atomic<std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>> *current_minimum_cost = nullptr,
                            std::vector<std::pair<ArgumentContainer, std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>>> *queries = nullptr);

    } // namespaces
``

The `turbo` function searches for a global minimum of a function on a box with TuRBO-1, the trust region Bayesian optimization algorithm of Eriksson, Pearce, Gardner, Turner and Poloczek.
It is meant for expensive functions: it spends milliseconds of its own time per function call, so that it can spend as few calls as possible.

TuRBO fits a Gaussian process (an ARD Matérn-5/2 kernel with a constant mean and a noise term) to the points it has evaluated,
but uses it only in a /trust region/: a box centred at the best point found so far, stretched along each coordinate in proportion to the lengthscale the Gaussian process learned for it.
Each new point is the minimizer, over a few hundred candidate points in the trust region, of a random function drawn from the posterior (Thompson sampling).
The trust region doubles in size after three batches in a row that improve on its best point, and halves after ⌈max(4, /n/)\/ /q/⌉ batches in a row that do not, where /q/ is the batch size;
when it becomes too small, TuRBO forgets it and starts again from a new random design, so that the search as a whole is global.

TuRBO is a good choice when each function call takes much longer than a few milliseconds and the budget is a few hundred to a few thousand calls.
It finds good values quickly, but it does not converge to high accuracy:
the trust region never gets smaller than 2[super -7] of the box, and the Gaussian process treats differences in function values smaller than its noise level as noise.
For cheap functions, or when the minimum is needed to many digits, multilevel coordinate search, differential evolution, jSO or CMA-ES are better choices,
or the result of TuRBO can be polished by a local method.

[heading Parameters]

* `lower_bounds`: A container representing the lower bounds of the optimization space along each dimension.
    The `.size()` of the bounds should return the dimension of the problem.
* `upper_bounds`: A container representing the upper bounds of the optimization space along each dimension.
    It should have the same size as `lower_bounds`, and each element must be strictly greater than the corresponding element of `lower_bounds`.
* `initial_guess`: An optional point in the box. It is the first function call, in place of the first point of the first random design.
* `max_function_calls`: Defaults to 100 times the dimension.
* `initial_points`: The number of points of the random (Latin hypercube) design that starts each trust region. Defaults to twice the dimension.
* `batch_size`: The number of points /q/ proposed at a time, from independent Thompson samples of the same Gaussian process. Defaults to 1.
    The points of a batch could be evaluated in parallel, but `turbo` itself evaluates them one after the other.
* `candidates`: The number of candidate points in the trust region at which each Thompson sample is drawn.
    Defaults to min(100/n/, 1000). It must be at least `batch_size`.
* `max_training_points`: The Gaussian process is fitted to at most this many points of the current trust region, those nearest its centre. Defaults to 200.
* `hyperparameter_steps`: The number of steps of the Adam optimizer on the log marginal likelihood each time the Gaussian process is fitted. Defaults to 50.

`validate_turbo_parameters` throws a `std::domain_error` if the bounds or initial guess are invalid,
and a `std::invalid_argument` if `batch_size`, `candidates` or `max_training_points` is out of range.
`turbo` calls it before the first function evaluation.

[heading The function]

``
template <typename ArgumentContainer, class Func, class URBG>
ArgumentContainer turbo(const Func cost_function,
                        turbo_parameters<ArgumentContainer> const &params,
                        URBG &gen,
                        std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer> target_value
                          = std::numeric_limits<std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>>::quiet_NaN(),
                        std::atomic<bool> *cancellation = nullptr,
                        std::atomic<std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>> *current_minimum_cost = nullptr,
                        std::vector<std::pair<ArgumentContainer, std::invoke_result_t<Func, ArgumentContainer>>> *queries = nullptr)
``

Parameters:

* `cost_function`: The cost function to be minimized. A NaN return value is treated as +[infin].
* `params`: The parameters to the algorithm as described above.
* `gen`: A uniform random bit generator, like `std::mt19937_64`.
* `target_value`: An optional value that, if reached, stops the optimization.
    This is the most robust way to terminate the calculation, but in most cases the optimal value of the cost function is unknown.
        If it is, use it! Physical considerations can often be used to find optimal values for cost functions.
* `cancellation`: An optional atomic boolean to allow the user to stop the computation and gracefully return the best result found up to that point.
    It is checked before every function call.
* `current_minimum_cost`: An optional atomic variable to store the current minimum cost during optimization.
    This allows developers to (e.g.) plot the progress of the minimization over time and in conjunction with the `cancellation` argument allow halting the computation when the progress stagnates.
* `queries`: An optional vector to store intermediate results during optimization.
    This is useful for debugging and perhaps volume rendering of the objective function after the calculation is complete.

Returns:

The `ArgumentContainer` corresponding to the minimum cost found by the optimization.
If the computation is cancelled before the first function call, it is the midpoint of the box (or the initial guess).

[heading Termination]

As in the paper, there is no convergence criterion: TuRBO restarts until it is stopped.
The computation stops when the `target_value` is reached, when `max_function_calls` calls have been made, or when `cancellation` is set.
These are checked before every call, even in the middle of a batch, so `max_function_calls` is never exceeded.

[heading Cost]

Each iteration fits the Gaussian process, with `hyperparameter_steps` Cholesky factorizations and inverses of an /N/ x /N/ matrix,
where /N/ is the number of training points, and draws the Thompson samples, with a Cholesky factorization of the /M/ x /M/ posterior covariance of the /M/ candidates.
That is O(`hyperparameter_steps` /N/[super 3] + /M/[super 3] + /M/[super 2]/N/) operations per iteration.
A trust region lasts about 25/n/ function calls, so /N/ reaches the default cap of 200 only for /n/ of about 8 or more.
On an Apple M1 Pro core, an iteration with the default parameters takes about 1 ms for /n/ = 2, 4 ms for /n/ = 4, 8 ms for /n/ = 5, 12 ms for /n/ = 6, and 85 ms for /n/ = 10.
Setting `max_training_points` to 100 and `candidates` to 500 brings the last down to about 15 ms.
All of the linear algebra is hand-rolled, and done in double precision whatever the `Real` type of the arguments.

[heading Implementation notes]

The trust region, its update rules, the Thompson sampling, the Gaussian process and the restarts follow Section 2 of the paper and its supplement,
and the parameters follow the authors' code ([@https://github.com/uber-research/TuRBO github.com/uber-research/TuRBO], `turbo_1.py` and `gp.py`) where the two differ.
Each Gaussian process fit starts from the same hyperparameters (lengthscales 0.5, output scale 1, noise variance 0.005, mean 0),
and runs 50 steps of Adam with learning rate 0.1 on the negative log marginal likelihood, with analytic gradients,
over hyperparameters mapped to the real line by scaled sigmoids onto the intervals \[0.005, 2\] (lengthscales), \[0.05, 20\] (output scale) and \[0.0005, 0.2\] (noise variance), as GPyTorch does.
The inputs are scaled to the unit cube, and the function values standardized by their median and standard deviation.
The departures from the paper and the authors' code are:

* The failure tolerance is ⌈max(4, /n/)\/ /q/⌉, the noise variance bound 0.2, and a success an improvement by more than 10[super -3] times the magnitude of the best value in the trust region,
    as in the authors' code. The paper has ⌈/n/\/ /q/⌉, 0.1, and any improvement.
* The candidates are a random Latin hypercube in the trust region rather than a scrambled Sobol sequence, with each coordinate of each candidate kept at the centre of the trust region except with probability min(20\/n, 1), as in the paper.
    A candidate with no perturbed coordinate gets one, chosen from all /n/ coordinates (the authors' code never chooses the last).
* The default number of candidates is min(100/n/, 1000), not min(100/n/, 5000).
    The joint posterior sample is exact, by a Cholesky factorization of the candidates' covariance, which costs /M/[super 3]/3 operations;
    the authors' code also uses a Cholesky factorization up to 2000 candidates, and an approximate (Lanczos) factorization above that.
* The Gaussian process is fitted to at most `max_training_points` points of the trust region, those nearest its centre; the authors' code uses all of them.
    The cap rarely matters below 8 dimensions, where trust regions do not last that long, and it bounds the cost of a fit, which grows as the cube of the number of points.
* Values that are +[infin] (or NaN) are replaced, in the training set, by the largest finite value in it. If there is none, the batch is taken at random from the candidates.
* The initial guess, if any, replaces the first point of the first design.
* If the run stops in the middle of a batch, the trust region is not updated.

Only TuRBO-1, with a single trust region, is implemented; TuRBO-/m/ maintains /m/ trust regions and allocates each batch among them.
The paper's choice of centre for noisy functions (the point with the smallest posterior mean) is not implemented: the centre is always the best point evaluated, as in the authors' code.

[h4:examples Examples]

The Branin function has three global minima with value 0.397887 on the box \[-5, 10\]×\[0, 15\]:

``
#include <boost/math/optimization/turbo.hpp>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <iostream>
#include <random>

int main() {
    using boost::math::optimization::turbo;
    using boost::math::optimization::turbo_parameters;
    using ArgType = std::array<double, 2>;
    auto branin = [](ArgType const & v) {
        double const pi = 3.14159265358979323846;
        double x = v[0];
        double y = v[1];
        double t = y - 5.1/(4*pi*pi)*x*x + 5*x/pi - 6;
        return t*t + 10*(1 - 1/(8*pi))*std::cos(x) + 10;
    };
    turbo_parameters<ArgType> params;
    params.lower_bounds = {-5, 0};
    params.upper_bounds = {10, 15};
    params.max_function_calls = 200;
    std::mt19937_64 gen(42);
    auto x = turbo(branin, params, gen);
    std::cout << "f(" << x[0] << ", " << x[1] << ") = " << branin(x) << "\n";
}
``

After 200 calls the value is typically within a few times 10[super -4] of the minimum: over 30 seeds, the median distance was 3.7×10[super -4] and the largest 1.7×10[super -3].

[h4:references References]

* D. Eriksson, M. Pearce, J. Gardner, R. D. Turner and M. Poloczek, ['Scalable global optimization via local Bayesian optimization.] Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 32 (NeurIPS 2019). [@https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.01739 arXiv:1910.01739].

[endsect] [/section:turbo]
